{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_065589c33bf946160169bb39f806f08191987dc7407f1fb273", "created": 1773877752, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"Fertility Preservation Guidance (NCCN Patient Guidelines) for Adolescents and Young Adults\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on fertility preservation as a set of clinical actions and planning steps to maintain future reproductive options, commonly in the context of cancer treatment. The core entity (FERTILITY PRESERVATION) connects to an informational distribution hub (NCCN.ORG/PATIENTGUIDELINES) and an audience focus on adolescents and young adults, reinforced by the NCCN patient guideline resource specifically for Adolescent and Young Adult Cancer. Supporting roles in the network include fertility and reproductive specialists and fertility counselors, who guide patients through understanding risks and options prior to treatment, with the desire to have kids presented as a key motivation for pursuing preservation steps.\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"Fertility preservation is the hub concept linking clinical options, counseling roles, and patient-facing guidance resources\",\"explanation\":\"FERTILITY PRESERVATION is described as a set of actions and medical strategies aimed at keeping future reproductive options open—most often for individuals expecting fertility-impacting cancer treatment—and it is typically considered before therapy begins (though it may also be part of treatment planning depending on urgency and expected effects). The entity’s description outlines both the planning orientation (coordinating ahead of treatment start) and the purpose (supporting future possibility of biological children and informing care decisions when fertility impact matters). [Data: Entities (1495)]\\n\\nWithin the network, fertility preservation functions as the central connector to (1)